[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Nexus and Disciplined Agile (DA) Overviews"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Welcome to this technical tutorial on scaling Agile frameworks. As organizations grow, applying Scrum or basic Agile practices to a single team is often insufficient. To address enterprise-level complexity, frameworks like Nexus and Disciplined Agile (DA) provide structured yet flexible approaches to scale delivery."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Nexus Framework"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Nexus is a scaled software development framework designed by Scrum.org. It is built specifically to guide up to approximately three to nine Scrum teams (roughly 30-80 people) working on a single product to deliver a single, integrated Increment at least by the end of every Sprint."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Nexus Integration Team (NIT): Composed of a Product Owner, Scrum Master, and Nexus team members responsible for ensuring that a usable, integrated Increment is produced."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Artifacts: Consists of the Nexus Sprint Backlog, which maps dependencies transparently across teams, alongside the standard Product and Sprint Backlogs."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Events: Introduces scaled events such as Nexus Sprint Planning, Nexus Daily Scrum, Nexus Sprint Review, and Nexus Sprint Retrospective to manage cross-team dependencies."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Disciplined Agile (DA)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Disciplined Agile (DA), now managed by PMI, is not a framework itself, but a toolkit or 'process decision engine'. DA provides a comprehensive hybrid approach that helps organizations choose their way of working (WoW) depending on their specific context and phase of maturity."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Goal-Driven: DA focuses on the goals organizations need to achieve rather than strictly prescribing specific processes or roles."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility: Scales effortlessly from small teams to enterprise-wide transformations, integrating elements from Scrum, Kanban, Lean, and traditional management."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Four Layers: Includes Foundation, Disciplined DevOps, Value Streams, and Enterprise Agile."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparing Nexus and Disciplined Agile"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"While both frameworks scale Agile practices, they target different organizational needs. Nexus is prescriptive, tightly coupled with Scrum, and ideal for multiple teams building a single product increment. Disciplined Agile is descriptive, highly customizable, and covers the entire enterprise value stream."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Conceptual DA Decision Matrix mapping
const choiceOfWayOfWorking = {
  context: "Enterprise Transformation",
  frameworksSupported: ["Scrum", "Kanban", "Nexus", "SAFe"],
  selectionStrategy: "Context-driven goals"
};"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing between Nexus and Disciplined Agile depends entirely on your organizational context. If you are deeply committed to Scrum and need to sync multiple teams on a single product, Nexus offers a lightweight and direct path. If you require a toolset to design a customized process framework across diverse departments, Disciplined Agile provides unmatched breadth and depth."}]
